# Who may compose the list of every layer, and whether anybody else does. One row per law, with
# the templates that compose `layers/every_layer.sqlc` and are not declared as denominators.
#
# The list of every layer reaches everything
#   `layers/every_layer.sqlc` is every filed layer, which is what makes it the denominator a
#   `LEFT JOIN` can find a gap against. Once a relation composes it for any other reason, every
#   relation that reads that one takes in the whole list, and any law of the form "this rule's reach
#   is inside the reach of what it composes" holds without testing anything.
#
# One edge is enough
#   `composition/parts.sqlc` is composed by more relations than anything else here, so an inner join
#   of the list there would put the whole list among the relations most of the checks compose. A
#   membership test reads `pm.layer`; the list is for denominators. Same rows, opposite roles, and
#   only one of the two is safe to pass on.
#
# The three roles
#   A blanket rule would forbid something correct. A denominator drives the query or is left-joined,
#   and that is what this relation allows. A membership test asks whether one pair exists and
#   belongs on the base table. And `entries/spillovers.sqlc` is a third: it joins the list to itself
#   with `filing <> filing` to find the same layer name in another filing, so its subject really is
#   the whole list. That is why the allowed set is declared here rather than read from the join
#   direction, which the graph of which template composes which cannot see.
#
# What it cannot see
#   It cannot see how an allowed template uses the list, only that it is allowed. So a file on this
#   list that starts inner-joining the list passes, and the law reaches as far as the list rather
#   than the usage. The join direction is in the SQL text, so the program that reads the text
#   classifies it and carries it in `public.compose_edge.inner_joins`, which keeps this list to the
#   real exceptions rather than every allowed template.
#
# One exception, by name
#   `inner_joins` carries the syntax, so the list is one row. Allowing templates by name would mean
#   adding every ordinary denominator use by hand, and a list like that goes stale on changes that
#   are fine. Driving from the list or left-joining it passes without being declared; only an inner
#   join needs a reason. `entries/spillovers.sqlc` is the one that has one, for the reason under the
#   three roles: the whole list really is its subject rather than a lookup.
-- algebra/roster.sqlc against public.compose_edge: who composes layers/every_layer.sqlc.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'layers/every_layer.sqlc' AS subject,
           count(*) = 0              AS holds,
           format('%s undeclared parent(s) compose the layer dimension: %s',
                  count(*), coalesce(string_agg(u.parent, ', ' ORDER BY u.parent), '(none)'))
                                     AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT e.parent
        FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_edges.sqlc) ) e
        WHERE e.child = 'layers/every_layer.sqlc'
          AND e.inner_joins > 0
          AND e.parent NOT IN ('entries/spillovers.sqlc')
    ) u
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'dimension_use'
